\begin{cases}
2x - y = 3 \\
x + y = 1
\end{cases}
\] Adding these two equations will eliminate \( y \) and allow us to solve for \( x \). Adding \((2x - y) + (x + y) = 3 + 1\) gives us \(3x = 4\), so \(x = \frac{4}{3}\). Next, we substitute \(x = \frac{4}{3}\) into the second equation to solve for \( y \): \[
x + y = 1 \implies \frac{4}{3} + y = 1 \implies y = 1 - \frac{4}{3} = -\frac{1}{3}
\] Now that we have \( x = \frac{4}{3} \) and \( y = -\frac{1}{3} \), we can compute \( 8x - 7y \): \[
8x - 7y = 8\left(\frac{4}{3}\right) - 7\left(-\frac{1}{3}\right) = \frac{32}{3} + \frac{7}{3} = \frac{39}{3} = 13
\] </think> <answer>13</answer>

